---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Media from '../../../components/Media.astro';
import Picture from '../../../components/Picture.astro';
import MaterialCutComparison from '../../../components/MaterialCutComparison.astro';
import {url} from '../../../lib/urls';
import {renderField,renderTrace,traceCaption} from '../../../../public/scripts/sdf-story.js';
const path='/demos/sdf/inside-a-solid/';
const source='https://github.com/byebyebryan/cubey/blob/main/';
const milestones=[
  {id:'history-sdf-carved-sphere',label:'01 · Procedural cuts',title:'Start with a simple question.',alt:'Historical native prototype: a gold sphere broken into a dense pattern by 4,096 procedural cuts.',text:'What happens when thousands of small solids add to, or cut away from, a shape? Stick forms and carved spheres exercised the field, traversal and saved recipes before there was a character to build.',link:source+'docs/notes/sdf-procedural-prototype-closeout.md',linkText:'Prototype record',historical:true},
  {id:'robot',label:'02 · Authored construction',title:'Give the operations a purpose.',alt:'The finished field-kit robot in a native SDF render.',text:'The robot turned those operations into assemblies: a torso, limbs, joints and a field kit. Its construction history made the composition inspectable. A later joint review corrected the relationship between the modeled hinges and the pose.',link:'/demos/sdf/robot/',linkText:'Explore the robot'},
  {id:'guardian',label:'03 · Sculptural strokes',title:'Try a different vocabulary.',alt:'The organic mirrored Guardian bust in restrained marble, rendered natively.',text:'Rounded strokes and mirrored edits made softer forms possible. Guardian’s 65 grouped operations contain 1,011 primitive segments. Several sculpture directions were tried; driftwood was retired, and the accepted Guardian settled on limestone and subtle marble.',link:'/demos/sdf/guardian/',linkText:'Explore Guardian'},
  {id:'mesh-materials',label:'04 · Shared materials',title:'Let the experiment feed the engine.',alt:'Twelve material spheres in the forward PBR workshop under dusk lighting.',text:'Material recipes moved into the shared foundation, where forward PBR and SDF workshops can use the same twelve-material library. Atmosphere and clouds now give both a common setting for looking at those surfaces.',link:'/demos/materials/',linkText:'Compare the material workshops'},
];
---
<Layout title="Building and cutting a solid" description="From a robot’s construction to the grain inside a cut: a visual look at Cubey’s signed-distance fields, with interactive illustrations and native renders." path={path}>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href={url('/styles/sdf-story.css',path)}/>
  <article class="sdf-story">
    <header class="solid-hero">
      <div><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href={url('/demos/',path)}>Demos</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href={url('/demos/sdf/',path)}>SDF forms</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">A closer look</span></nav><p class="eyebrow">Inside the experiment</p><h1>Building and cutting a solid.</h1><p class="intro">A robot assembled from simple shapes. A cut that reveals the grain inside. Both begin with a way to describe a solid beyond its visible skin.</p></div>
      <div class="solid-hero-film"><Media entry="robot" path={path} title="Robot construction" eager caption="Native SDF render · saved construction replay, edited into a short film."/><p class="note">The film replays authored steps. Continuous brush editing is a separate, deferred experiment.</p></div>
    </header>
    <nav class="chapter-nav" aria-label="In this story"><a href="#inside">Look inside</a><a href="#combine">Combine &amp; cut</a><a href="#find">Find the surface</a><a href="#journey">How it developed</a></nav>

    <section class="section solid-inside" id="inside" aria-labelledby="inside-title"><div class="solid-section-head"><div><p class="number">01 · Look inside</p><h2 id="inside-title">The material continues through the cut.</h2></div><p>Wood grain and marble veins are evaluated through a volume. Cutting a new face exposes another part of that field, so the pattern continues across the outside and the new interior.</p></div><MaterialCutComparison path={path} initial="through-cut"/><p class="note">Native SDF captures · fixed camera, saved cut states. Rusted steel and clear glass extend the comparison. Glass uses the gallery’s environment-only light path: it traces its own interfaces and samples the environment, without refracting the other displayed bodies.</p><a class="text-link" href={url('/demos/materials/',path)}>See all twelve materials in forward PBR and SDF ↗</a></section>

    <section class="section solid-section" id="combine" aria-labelledby="combine-title">
      <div class="solid-copy"><p class="number">02 · Describe the solid</p><h2 id="combine-title">A surface is where the value reaches zero.</h2><p>A signed-distance field assigns a value to a position: negative inside a shape, positive outside, zero on its boundary. Simple shapes have compact distance functions.</p><p>Combine those functions to join solids or remove one from another. The new inside wall follows from the same description of the solid.</p><p class="note">Try the three stages, then move the cutting shape.</p><details><summary>A little of the math</summary><p>For distances <code>a</code> and <code>b</code>, a union uses <code>min(a, b)</code>; a subtraction uses <code>max(a, −b)</code>. These combinations preserve the sign and surface, but their magnitude is a conservative distance bound rather than the exact Euclidean distance everywhere.</p></details></div>
      <figure class="solid-figure" id="field-illustration" data-field-figure>
        <div class="solid-figure-label">Interactive illustration · 2D</div>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 400" role="img" aria-labelledby="field-title field-desc"><title id="field-title">Combining and subtracting distance fields</title><desc id="field-desc">Green cells are inside the solid, blue cells are outside. The pale line follows the zero boundary. A dashed coral circle marks the subtractor in the cut view.</desc><g data-field-drawing set:html={renderField()}/></svg>
        <div class="solid-legend"><span><i class="legend-inside" aria-hidden="true"></i> Inside −</span><span><i class="legend-boundary" aria-hidden="true"></i> Boundary 0</span><span><i class="legend-outside" aria-hidden="true"></i> Outside +</span></div>
        <div class="solid-controls" data-figure-controls hidden>
          <div class="solid-buttons" role="group" aria-label="Shape operation"><button type="button" class="filter" data-field-mode="circle" aria-pressed="false">Circle</button><button type="button" class="filter" data-field-mode="join" aria-pressed="false">Join a rectangle</button><button type="button" class="filter" data-field-mode="cut" aria-pressed="true">Cut a hole</button></div>
          <label class="solid-range" for="cut-position">Move the cutting shape<input id="cut-position" type="range" min="-.65" max=".85" step=".01" value=".22"/></label>
        </div>
        <figcaption><p class="solid-readout" data-field-caption aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">Remove the circular region. Its boundary becomes a new inside wall.</p><p class="note">An illustrative field and sampled contour. This diagram does not edit a native Cubey model.</p></figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="section solid-section" id="find" aria-labelledby="find-title">
      <div class="solid-copy"><p class="number">03 · Find the surface</p><h2 id="find-title">Use the distance to choose the next step.</h2><p>Send a ray from the camera. At each sample, the distance tells it how far it can advance towards a possible surface. Far away, it can take a large step; near the boundary, the steps shrink.</p><p>Here, a circle gives an exact distance. Step through the ray by hand, or raise it until it misses. The thin green circle shows the distance available at the current sample.</p><p class="note">“Close enough” here is a small illustrative tolerance. The units and step count are unrelated to native frame timings.</p></div>
      <figure class="solid-figure" id="ray-illustration" data-trace-figure>
        <div class="solid-figure-label">Interactive illustration · analytic circle</div>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 420" role="img" aria-labelledby="ray-title ray-desc"><title id="ray-title">A ray stepping towards a circle</title><desc id="ray-desc">The dashed ray travels from left to right. A pale dot marks the current sample; an outlined circle shows its distance to the solid. Coral dots and a line record completed steps.</desc><g data-trace-drawing set:html={renderTrace()}/></svg>
        <div class="solid-controls" data-figure-controls hidden><div class="solid-buttons"><button type="button" class="filter" data-trace-next>Next step →</button><button type="button" class="filter" data-trace-reset>Reset steps</button></div><label class="solid-range" for="ray-height">Ray height<input id="ray-height" type="range" min="-.9" max=".9" step=".025" value=".25"/></label></div>
        <figcaption><p class="solid-readout" data-trace-caption aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">{traceCaption(.25,0)}</p><p class="note">A 2D explanation of distance-guided stepping, separate from the native renderer below.</p></figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="section solid-cache" aria-labelledby="cache-title"><div class="solid-copy"><p class="eyebrow">In Cubey</p><h2 id="cache-title">Keep the detail near the surface.</h2><p>The native renderer does more than evaluate a circle. Cubey builds a hierarchy of the solid and stores small 3D grids of distance samples near its surface. Rays skip coarse empty regions, then interpolate those stored samples to find a hit.</p></div><ol class="solid-pipeline"><li><span>01</span><strong>Author</strong><p>Shapes and operations describe the solid.</p></li><li><span>02</span><strong>Build</strong><p>A hierarchy and near-surface sample bricks cache the field.</p></li><li><span>03</span><strong>Trace</strong><p>Traverse cells and sample the field to find the visible boundary.</p></li><li><span>04</span><strong>Shade</strong><p>Estimate the normal, evaluate the material, then light the surface.</p></li></ol><p class="note">The stored field is an approximation. Sampling, hit tolerances and grazing silhouettes matter; the illustration above is not a simulation of the native brick tracer. Geometry edits currently rebuild the field synchronously. Material changes can reuse that geometry cache.</p></section>

    <section class="section" id="journey" aria-labelledby="journey-title"><div class="solid-section-head"><div><p class="number">04 · How it developed</p><h2 id="journey-title">One experiment made room for the next.</h2></div><p>The first question was how to render and inspect these fields. A robot, a sculpture and a material workshop each put a different kind of pressure on that foundation.</p></div><div class="solid-milestones">{milestones.map(m=><section class="solid-milestone"><Picture id={m.id} path={path} alt={m.alt}/><p class="eyebrow">{m.label}</p><h3>{m.title}</h3>{m.historical&&<p class="solid-history-label">Historical native capture · procedural closeout</p>}<p>{m.text}</p><a class="text-link" href={url(m.link,path)}>{m.linkText} ↗</a></section>)}</div><p class="note">The saved histories make construction inspectable. They do not qualify interactive sculpting: continuous brushes, incremental field updates and deeper light transport remain separate experiments.</p></section>

    <section class="section solid-sources"><div><p class="eyebrow">Open the workshop</p><h2>Follow the implementation.</h2></div><div><p>The diagrams explain the ideas. The source and experiment records show the representation, fixes and limits behind the native results.</p><div class="source-strip"><a href={source+'projects/sdf/README.md'}>SDF guide &amp; recipes ↗</a><a href={source+'projects/sdf/shaders/trace.glsl'}>Native tracing shader ↗</a><a href={source+'docs/notes/sdf-thread-closeout.md'}>Experiment record ↗</a><a href={url('/credits/',path)}>Capture credits ↗</a></div><a class="text-link" href={url('/demos/sdf/',path)}>Back to SDF forms ↗</a></div></section>
  </article>
  <script is:inline type="module" src={url('/scripts/sdf-story.js',path)}></script>
</Layout>
